{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: the excerpt boundary can split a UTF-16 surrogate pair**

`file.content.substring(0, excerptBytes)` can cut between the two code units of an astral character (emoji, rare CJK) that straddles the boundary — the model-facing excerpt would end in a lone surrogate, which UTF-8 encoders render as U+FFFD replacement junk right before the MUST-read instruction.

This is a pre-existing pattern (the old budget code `substring`'d the same way), so not a regression — but this code now feeds the system prompt for arbitrary user content, so a cheap boundary guard would be a nice hardening:

```dart
var cut = excerptBytes;
if (cut > 0 && cut < file.content.length) {
  final prev = file.content.codeUnitAt(cut - 1);
  if (prev >= 0xD800 && prev <= 0xDBFF) cut--; // land inside a surrogate pair
}
```

(one code unit of budget slop, no behavioral change for ASCII/MD files).
